The contract is an object literal checked with `satisfies`, so it keeps the precise type of every schema while still being validated against the general shape:

```ts
import { z } from "zod";

type Endpoint = {
  method: "GET" | "POST" | "PATCH" | "DELETE";
  path: `/${string}`;
  query?: z.ZodType;
  body?: z.ZodType;
  response: z.ZodType;
  errors?: Partial<Record<400 | 401 | 403 | 404 | 409 | 422, z.ZodType>>;
};
type Contract = Record<string, Endpoint>;

export const contract = {
  getProject: {
    method: "GET",
    path: "/projects/:projectId",
    response: ProjectSchema,
    errors: { 404: NotFoundSchema },
  },
  createTask: {
    method: "POST",
    path: "/projects/:projectId/tasks",
    body: CreateTaskSchema,
    response: TaskSchema,
    errors: { 422: ValidationErrorSchema },
  },
} as const satisfies Contract;
```

Why `as const satisfies` rather than an annotation: `const contract: Contract = ...` would widen every `path` to `` `/${string}` `` and every schema to `z.ZodType`, destroying the information the client needs. `satisfies` checks the shape (a typo in `method` or a missing `response` is an error) while `as const` keeps literal paths for the type-level router.

Derived types use small named helpers so hovers stay readable and the checker caches them:

```ts
type Def = typeof contract;
type ResponseOf<K extends keyof Def> = z.infer<Def[K]["response"]>;
type BodyOf<K extends keyof Def> = Def[K] extends { body: infer B extends z.ZodType } ? z.input<B> : never;
```

Note `z.input` for bodies: the caller supplies the wire shape, and transforms (string to Date) run during the parse.

Path params come from the path string itself, so they cannot get out of sync:

```ts
type PathParams<P extends string> =
  P extends `${string}:${infer Name}/${infer Rest}` ? { [K in Name | keyof PathParams<Rest>]: string } :
  P extends `${string}:${infer Name}` ? { [K in Name]: string } :
  Record<never, never>;

type ParamsArg<P extends string> = keyof PathParams<P> extends never
  ? { params?: undefined }
  : { params: PathParams<P> };
```

`ParamsArg` makes `params` required exactly when the path has parameters. At runtime the client fills the path with a regex replace and `encodeURIComponent` per value, and throws if a placeholder remains, which can only happen through a cast.

Testing the types is part of the milestone:

```ts
expectTypeOf<PathParams<"/projects/:projectId/tasks/:taskId">>()
  .toEqualTypeOf<{ projectId: string; taskId: string }>();
// @ts-expect-error missing taskId
client.call("getTask", { params: { projectId: "p1" } });
```

Limits are documented: recursion depth is tiny (paths have a handful of segments), and optional or wildcard segments are out of scope rather than half supported.

Throwing loses type information: `catch (e)` is `unknown`. The client returns values instead:

```ts
type ApiError<K extends keyof Def> =
  | { kind: "http"; status: number; body: ErrorBodyOf<K> }
  | { kind: "validation"; where: "request" | "response"; issues: { path: string; message: string }[] }
  | { kind: "network"; cause: unknown }
  | { kind: "timeout"; ms: number }
  | { kind: "aborted" };

type Result<T, E> = { ok: true; data: T } | { ok: false; error: E };
```

Each endpoint's declared error schemas type `body` per status where they exist. A response that fails its schema is a `validation` error with `where: "response"`, which is logged with issue paths only (never the payload) because it signals contract drift, not user error.

In the UI, an `ApiErrorView` switches on `error.kind` with an `assertNever` default, so adding a variant forces every error view to handle it. The TanStack Query wrapper throws a typed error object for `useQuery`'s error state, and the hook's `error` is typed accordingly rather than `Error`.
